WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.667
This practice asks you to compare two valid but different policies.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:07.922
The first filter uses ordinary not-equal.

00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:14.387
A missing payment status does not produce TRUE when compared with Paid; it produces UNKNOWN,

00:00:14.387 --> 00:00:16.347
so the row is removed.

00:00:16.347 --> 00:00:19.502
The second filter uses IS DISTINCT FROM.

00:00:19.502 --> 00:00:24.537
That gives a definite comparison, so NULL is considered distinct from Paid and remains in the result.

00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:29.787
The extra record is the one with missing payment status.

00:00:29.787 --> 00:00:33.217
Now translate both queries into business language.

00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:38.277
The first means: show me records with a known status that is not Paid.

00:00:38.277 --> 00:00:42.342
The second means: show me records that are not definitely Paid,

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:46.482
including records whose status is missing and may need review.

00:00:46.482 --> 00:00:50.782
Both interpretations can be correct depending on the purpose.

00:00:50.782 --> 00:00:58.097
A collections team might want the second because missing status is a risk that requires investigation.

00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:03.432
A clean operational report might want only known non-Paid statuses.

00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:07.322
This is the kind of choice an analyst needs to communicate.

00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:10.827
SQL should not decide the business policy for you.

00:01:10.827 --> 00:01:14.737
The query should represent a policy that was already understood.
